/* std/mem, allocation primitives (M6.2).

   The only thing here that Kelp cannot express is the allocation itself, so that
   is the one `extern`: `alloc_raw`'s body is supplied by the compiler and
   produces an owned, zero-filled byte buffer. Everything a caller sees is the
   ordinary function below it, written in Kelp and reviewable as such.

   There is deliberately no `mem.free`: owned values are freed by the drop
   rules, so an explicit free would be a double free. */

extern fun alloc_raw(n: int) => Bytes

/// An owned, zero-filled buffer of `n` bytes. This allocator's result is
/// fixed-size; the buffers that *grow* are `String`/`Bytes`, which own their
/// own storage and reserve as they append (S1).
fun alloc(n: int) => Bytes = alloc_raw(n)
